---
import BlogArticleShell from '../../../components/BlogArticleShell.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const canonicalPath = '/blog/mixing-categories-outbound-parcel-spreadsheet-2026';
const publishedISO = '2026-07-01';
---

<BlogArticleShell
  title="Mixing categories in one outbound parcel: Kakobuy Spreadsheet routing (2026) | kakobuy-cn.com"
  description="Sneakers plus perfume plus electronics in one carton? A 2026 editorial guide to mixed-category consolidation on Kakobuy—route restrictions, volumetric maths, and when to split parcels."
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  headline="Mixing categories in one parcel: outbound routing for Kakobuy Spreadsheet hauls (2026)"
  publishedISO={publishedISO}
  displayDate="1 July 2026"
  structuredKeywords={[
    'mixed category Kakobuy parcel',
    'consolidation routing 2026',
    'spreadsheet mixed haul freight',
    'agent line restrictions reps',
    'kakobuy-cn.com outbound guide',
  ]}
>
  <p class="blog-lede" slot="lede">
    Spreadsheet shopping rarely stays in one lane—you paste sneakers from <a href="/category/shoes/">Shoes</a>, a belt from <a href="/category/accessories/">Accessories</a>, maybe perfume from a deep pool—then expect one outbound carton to “just work.”
    Mixed-category consolidation on the <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet</a></strong> workflow is where freight quotes fail, routes reject branded leather next to batteries, and volumetric weight surprises appear.
    This guide is editorial context for <strong>2026</strong> routing decisions—not agent policy gospel.
  </p>

  <h2>Why mixed carts break assumptions</h2>
  <div class="blog-table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Conflict type</th><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">Typical outcome</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Route restriction</td><td>Perfume + electronics</td><td>Line unavailable or seized risk</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Branded routing</td><td>LV bag + unbranded tees</td><td>Stricter line required for whole carton</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Volumetric mismatch</td><td>Puffer + slides + tees</td><td>One SKU dominates chargeable weight</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Customs description</td><td>Mixed trademark goods</td><td>Higher scrutiny—<a href="/blog/kakobuy-customs-declared-value-primer/">declared value</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Safe-ish mixing patterns</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Apparel-only:</strong> hoodies, tees, pants, headwear—similar restrictions, compress well.</li>
    <li><strong>Footwear + apparel:</strong> common; watch shoe boxes—<a href="/blog/shoe-box-keep-or-drop-consolidation-2026/">box guide</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Small leather + apparel:</strong> belts, cardholders—confirm branded line eligibility.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Lane guides for building: <a href="/blog/kakobuy-hoodies-sweatshirts-spreadsheet-lane-2026/">hoodies</a>, <a href="/blog/kakobuy-belts-spreadsheet-lane-guide-2026/">belts</a>, <a href="/blog/kakobuy-sneakers-spreadsheet-lane-guide-2026/">sneakers</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>When to split into two parcels</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Batteries or electronics</strong>—<a href="/blog/shipping-electronics-batteries-iata-2026-rules/">IATA 2026 rules</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Perfume / aerosol</strong>—<a href="/blog/kakobuy-perfume-fragrance-spreadsheet-lane-2026/">perfume lane</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>One volumetric monster</strong>—parka billing alone may beat consolidated “savings.”</li>
    <li><strong>Branded vs unbranded split</strong>—<a href="/blog/shipping-branded-vs-unbranded-spreadsheet-restrictions/">branded shipping</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Packaging &amp; rehearsal tactics</h2>
  <p>
    Mixed carts need deliberate void fill—not random seller boxes stacked.
    Request <a href="/blog/kakobuy-rehearsal-packaging-freight/">rehearsal packaging</a> with measured dims before paying freight.
    Summer lightweight mixes: <a href="/blog/summer-lightweight-haul-freight-2026/">summer freight tips</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Customs &amp; declared value honesty</h2>
  <p>
    One parcel = one declaration story.
    Under-declaring a branded bag because tees are “low value” invites holds.
    EU buyers: <a href="/blog/eu-import-vat-landed-cost-spreadsheet-buyers/">EU VAT</a>; AU: <a href="/blog/australia-customs-kakobuy-spreadsheet-import-2026/">Australia GST</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Workflow checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Plan cart lanes on <strong><a href="/">Kakobuy Spreadsheet home</a></strong> before paste spree.</li>
    <li>Flag restriction categories early (perfume, batteries, down parkas).</li>
    <li>QC approve all items before consolidation request.</li>
    <li>Rehearse dims; compare single vs split quotes—<a href="/blog/kakobuy-freight-line-picker-spreadsheet-2026/">line picker</a>.</li>
    <li>Ship with honest contents description and value buffer.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Group hauls: <a href="/blog/group-haul-consolidation-freight-savings/">consolidation savings</a>.
    Parcel checklist: <a href="/blog/kakobuy-spreadsheet-to-parcel-checklist/">spreadsheet to parcel</a>.
  </p>
</BlogArticleShell>
